# 14. Basic Port Scan

Example:

```bash
nmap 192.168.56.101
```

Nmap checks its default set of commonly used ports.

Example result:

```text
PORT     STATE  SERVICE
22/tcp   open   ssh
80/tcp   open   http
443/tcp  open   https
```

Interpretation:

- Port 22 → SSH is available
- Port 80 → HTTP is available
- Port 443 → HTTPS is available

---

# 15. Scan Specific Ports

You can specify ports.

Example:

```bash
nmap -p 22,80,443 192.168.56.101
```

This checks only:

```text
22
80
443
```

---

# 16. Scan a Port Range

Example:

```bash
nmap -p 1-1000 192.168.56.101
```

This scans ports:

```text
1 through 1000
```

---

# 17. Scan All TCP Ports

The `-p-` option tells Nmap to scan all TCP ports.

Example:

```bash
nmap -p- 192.168.56.101
```

Conceptually:

```text
1 → 65535
```

### Memory

> **-p- = All TCP ports**

---

# 18. TCP Connect Scan

Option:

```bash
nmap -sT 192.168.56.101
```

This performs a TCP connect scan.

The operating system completes the normal TCP connection.

### Concept

```text
Client → SYN
Server → SYN/ACK
Client → ACK
```

---

# 19. SYN Scan

Option:

```bash
nmap -sS 192.168.56.101
```

This is commonly called a **TCP SYN scan**.

It uses TCP SYN behavior to determine port states without completing a normal application connection in the usual way.

### Concept

```text
Tester → SYN
Target → SYN/ACK
Tester → RST
```

### Memory

> **-sS = SYN Scan**

---

# 20. SYN Scan vs Connect Scan

| SYN Scan | Connect Scan |
|---|---|
| `-sS` | `-sT` |
| Uses SYN-based probing | Uses full TCP connection |
| Common default when privileges allow | Uses OS connect call |
| Usually efficient | More connection-oriented |

---

# 21. UDP Scan

Option:

```bash
nmap -sU 192.168.56.101
```

Used to investigate UDP ports.

Examples of UDP-based services include:

- DNS
- DHCP
- SNMP

### Important

UDP scanning can be slower and results may be less straightforward because UDP does not use a TCP handshake.

---

# 22. TCP vs UDP

### TCP

```text
Connection-oriented
```

### UDP

```text
Connectionless
```

### Memory

> **TCP = Connection**

> **UDP = No connection**

---

# 23. Host Discovery

Before scanning ports, you may want to determine which hosts are reachable.

Example:

```bash
nmap -sn 192.168.56.0/24
```

This performs host discovery without a normal port scan.

### Memory

> **-sn = Host Discovery**

---

# 24. Service Detection

Nmap can attempt to identify the service running on an open port.

Option:

```bash
nmap -sV 192.168.56.101
```

Example:

```text
PORT     STATE SERVICE
22/tcp   open  ssh
80/tcp   open  http
```

With service detection, Nmap may provide additional product/version information.

---

# 25. Version Detection

`-sV` attempts to determine:

- Service
- Product
- Version
- Sometimes additional service details

Example concept:

```text
22/tcp open ssh OpenSSH ...
```

### Why useful?

Software versions can help security testers determine which security checks may be relevant.

### Important

> **Version detection does not automatically prove a vulnerability.**

---

# 26. OS Detection

Option:

```bash
nmap -O 192.168.56.101
```

Nmap attempts to identify the target's operating system using network behavior.

Possible results may include:

```text
Windows
Linux
Network appliance
```

### Important

OS detection is an **inference**, not guaranteed fact.

---

# 27. Aggressive Scan

Option:

```bash
nmap -A 192.168.56.101
```

It enables several advanced detection capabilities.

It may include:

- OS detection
- Version detection
- Script scanning
- Traceroute

### Important

Because it generates more traffic and performs more checks, use it only when appropriate for the authorized assessment.

### Memory

> **-A = Aggressive/Advanced detection**

---

# 28. NSE – Nmap Scripting Engine

**NSE = Nmap Scripting Engine**

NSE allows Nmap to use scripts for additional discovery and security auditing.

Scripts can assist with:

- Service enumeration
- Protocol discovery
- Configuration checks
- Authentication-related checks
- Vulnerability checks
- Information gathering

---

# 29. NSE Script Categories

NSE scripts are organized into categories such as:

- `auth`
- `broadcast`
- `brute`
- `default`
- `discovery`
- `dos`
- `exploit`
- `external`
- `fuzzer`
- `intrusive`
- `malware`
- `safe`
- `version`
- `vuln`

### Important

Not every category is equally appropriate for every engagement.

Some scripts can be intrusive or disruptive.

---

# 30. Default NSE Scripts

Option:

```bash
nmap -sC 192.168.56.101
```

Runs Nmap's default script set.

Equivalent long-form concept:

```text
--script=default
```

### Memory

> **-sC = Default Scripts**

---

# 31. Specific NSE Script

You can specify a script when appropriate.

Concept:

```bash
nmap --script=<script-name> <authorized-target>
```

Example using a safe discovery-style script:

```bash
nmap --script=http-title 192.168.56.101
```

This can help retrieve the HTTP page title from an authorized web service.

---

# 32. Vulnerability Scripts

Nmap also provides scripts categorized for vulnerability checking.

Example syntax:

```bash
nmap --script=vuln 192.168.56.101
```

### Important

Use vulnerability scripts carefully.

They can generate additional traffic and some checks may be intrusive.

### Important Exam Point

> **NSE vulnerability results should be validated; an Nmap script result is not automatically proof of a vulnerability.**

---

# 33. Script Categories vs Script Names

Do not confuse:

```text
--script=vuln
```

with:

```text
--script=http-title
```

### First

`vuln` = category

### Second

`http-title` = individual script

---

# 34. Output Formats

Nmap can save results for later analysis.

### Normal output

```bash
nmap -oN scan.txt 192.168.56.101
```

### XML output

```bash
nmap -oX scan.xml 192.168.56.101
```

### All major output formats

```bash
nmap -oA scan 192.168.56.101
```

This creates multiple output formats using the same base name.

### Memory

> **-oN = Normal**

> **-oX = XML**

> **-oA = All major formats**

---

# 35. Verbose Mode

Option:

```bash
nmap -v 192.168.56.101
```

Provides more detailed progress/output.

Higher verbosity can be requested with additional `v` levels where supported.

### Memory

> **-v = Verbose**

---

# 36. Timing

Nmap provides timing templates such as:

```text
-T0
-T1
-T2
-T3
-T4
-T5
```

Generally:

```text
T0 → Very slow
T1 → Slow
T2 → Polite
T3 → Normal
T4 → Aggressive
T5 → Insane
```

### Important

Faster scans can create more traffic and may be more noticeable.

Use timing appropriate to the authorized environment.

